findings.claim:
GC roots:
- the C stack, from mark_roots' frame (aligned) up to gc_bottom = @main's frame address + 16;
- callee-saved registers, spilled by __builtin_unwind_init in collect();
- @pys.roots, a private table that @main passes to pys_init, holding only the program's pointer-typed globals (int globals are not in it);
- runtime statics: ch1, args, and xr/unw once xthrow is set.
Without @main/pys_init, pmap is NULL (the first allocation that maps pages crashes) and gc_bottom is NULL (nothing on the stack is scanned).

Prototype fix, about 12 lines added to a scratch copy of runtime.c:
- pys_lib_init(roots,n): gc_init with no stack bottom, std streams, pys_eh_on, and no signal handlers or setvbuf;
- pys_enter(sb)/pys_leave(old): the outermost entry sets gc_bottom;
- pys_pin/pys_unpin: a malloc'd root array scanned in mark_roots.
Together with IR wrappers (frameaddress, enter/leave, try_mark, invoke, catch-all landingpad, exc_begin, exc_repr, exc_restore, error out-parameter), a real CPython 3.13 extension imported 50 frames deep passes under PYSTACHY_GC_STRESS=1: correct totals, pinned capsules intact, KeyError turned into a Python RuntimeError, and Ctrl-C (SIGINT) still reaches Python.

Program entry: @main calls llvm.frameaddress(0), then pys_init(argc, argv, sb, @pys.roots, n), then pys_eh_on() only if the program has a try statement (Gen.eh is a closed-world flag), then @main.init (which runs @init.<mod> for imports), then pys_finish(), and returns 0. pys_init also:
- builds sys.argv (and honours PYSTACHY_ARGV0, resetting crash signal handlers under lli);
- wraps stdout in an 8 KiB pending buffer plus setvbuf when it is not a tty;
- ignores SIGPIPE;
- installs a SIGINT handler.
A library entry needs everything but argv/signals/stdout, with eh forced on, the module init run once, and pys_finish registered at exit.

Effect letters for an unknown foreign function: conservatively U, which is FXALL (every letter but N: R A U I rL wL rD wD rO wO rG wG rF wF). The compiler already assigns this to any call of a callee it did not compile. Narrower sets:
- a pure leaf that touches no Pystachy memory: none (like fabs);
- I/O or process/global state: I;
- may raise through pys_fail/pys_raise: R (and N if it never returns);
- allocates Pystachy objects: A;
- reads or writes containers passed to it: rL/wL/rD/wD;
- calls back into Pystachy code: U.
R makes a call inside a try an invoke (eh_ir).

Issue #4 §4 sets requirements for any boundary before implementation:
- an ADR comparing extension mode, explicit opaque-Python-object bridging and expansion of the independent runtime;
- a spec of identity and shared mutation, owned and borrowed references, error propagation, callbacks, buffer protocols and cross-runtime cycles;
- mutable Python objects stay Python objects by default, with explicit copies only;
- zero-copy numerical buffers come first;
- explicit eligibility diagnostics, with no invisible performance cliffs;
- completion gate: one pure-Python and one native-extension package run unchanged, and baselines are taken against mypyc, Cython and CinderX.

implications_for_ffi:
A wrapper-style extension that links runtime.c into the .so needs:
- a GC root window set at each entry;
- a root table for Pystachy objects referenced from CPython;
- a catch-all landing pad at every exported entry, converting to PyErr, because forced unwinding would otherwise skip CPython frames;
- no exit() on errors;
- no pys_init signal or stdout takeover inside a host interpreter.

Bidirectional calls (Pystachy → CPython → Pystachy) need four things the docs don't have yet:
- functions as values (M3) for callbacks;
- a refcount release hook at sweep, modeled on how files are closed at collection, since __del__ never runs;
- a policy for cross-runtime cycles;
- a mapping of arbitrary Python exceptions into the closed exception-class world.
